WEBVTT

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:09.430
Here’s the tricky part about running these conversations with your own team: you’re wearing two hats at once.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.910
As the leader, you have opinions, responsibility and power.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:21.650
As the facilitator, your job is to help the team think together, and to make sure every voice is heard, not just the loudest one.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.090
And definitely not just yours.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:29.290
The risk is simple. When the leader speaks first, the conversation is over.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.730
People adjust to what you said, often without noticing.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.950
So the first rule is: speak last.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.970
Ask the question, then wait. Let others go first.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.010
Add your view at the end, and frame it as one view among others.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:51.290
There is one exception: when you ask people to share something personal, like a story or their user manual, go first.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.790
There you’re not giving an opinion; you’re showing how open it’s safe to be.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.810
The second rule is to name which hat you’re wearing.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:04.790
Say it out loud: “Now I’m taking off my facilitator hat for a moment, and speaking as your manager.”

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.810
It sounds a bit formal the first time.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:12.250
But it helps people a lot, because they know when you’re guiding the process and when you’re giving direction.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:16.170
Third: be clear about what is open and what is not.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.730
If some things are already decided, say so up front.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:24.610
Nothing damages trust faster than inviting people to shape something, and then overruling them.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:30.770
“We decide this together” and “I decide, but I want your input” are both fine.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.950
Just be honest about which one it is.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.210
And finally, know when to hand the facilitator hat to someone else.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:47.030
If the topic is about you, your leadership, or a conflict you’re part of, ask a colleague, someone from HR, or an outside facilitator to guide that conversation.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.070
Then you can take part fully, as a team member.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:54.030
Wearing two hats is a skill, and it gets easier with practice.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.281
Most teams appreciate it deeply when a leader steps back and lets them think.

